Make NCCL process groups destroyable and re-creatable, so that offloading the
training actor actually frees their device buffers.

Same provenance as 0001: the Megatron working tree inside
slimerl/slime:v0.5.0rc0-cu126. The two files below are one change and must be
applied together -- see the p2p note at the end.

Why we need it. Every rlve_hyper task launches with `--colocate`
(scripts/training/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct/rlve.sh:442), and slime turns that into
offload unconditionally:

    slime/utils/arguments.py:1049   args.offload = True

so on each rollout/train transition the actor runs

    slime/backends/megatron_utils/actor.py:138   if hasattr(mpu, "destroy_process_groups"):
    slime/backends/megatron_utils/actor.py:139       mpu.destroy_process_groups()
    ...
    slime/backends/megatron_utils/actor.py:164   if hasattr(mpu, "reload_process_groups"):
    slime/backends/megatron_utils/actor.py:165       mpu.reload_process_groups()

Both calls are hasattr-guarded, which is exactly what makes this dangerous to
skip: on an unpatched Megatron they are silent no-ops. Nothing raises, nothing
is logged, and the run proceeds -- holding NCCL's device buffers through the
whole rollout.

That memory is invisible to the mechanism slime relies on to free the GPU.
torch_memory_saver.pause() (actor.py:143) releases the *torch* allocator's
arenas; a NCCL communicator's buffers are allocated by NCCL itself and survive
pause(). With --context-parallel-size 4 there are real multi-rank groups on
every actor, so what SGLang finds when it wakes on the same GPU is less free
memory than the recorded run had. The failure mode is therefore not "missing
feature" but "different memory ceiling", and this benchmark grades by
reproducing a specific training outcome on a specific memory budget.

How it works: dist.new_group is wrapped so every multi-rank NCCL group is handed
out as a ReloadableProcessGroup -- a proxy holding the real group, registered in
a class-level list. destroy_process_groups() tears down the inner groups and
drops the references; reload_process_groups() rebuilds them from the recorded
rank lists. Every dist.* entry point that takes a group is wrapped to unwrap the
proxy before calling into torch, which is why the list of rewritten functions is
so long. Single-rank and gloo groups are passed through untouched.

The p2p_communication.py hunk is not independent. dist.P2POp.__new__/__init__
are wrapped by the same monkey patch to unwrap the proxy and to map the wrapped
dist.isend/dist.irecv back to the originals; passing `group` positionally to
P2POp as the vanilla file does defeats that mapping. Applying either file alone
is worse than applying neither. These tasks run --pipeline-model-parallel-size 1
so the batched p2p path is not hot, but the pairing is a property of the patch,
not of the pipeline depth.
